def _is_safe_to_autoreply(sender, text, is_vip):
    """
    The actual gate for whether screen_message may send a reply on its
    own instead of asking first. ALL of the following must hold:
      1. Sender is an explicitly opted-in auto-reply contact (see
         add_auto_reply_contact_cmd) -- never inferred, always a
         deliberate customer choice about a specific known person.
      2. Sender is NOT flagged VIP -- VIP status specifically means
         "always tell me when they text," which is a stronger,
         explicit signal than routine-auto-reply eligibility; VIP wins.
      3. The message doesn't read as urgent (classify_urgency).
      4. The message doesn't read as high-stakes (classify_high_stakes)
         -- financial, legal, medical, or another consequential topic.
    Fails closed (False) on any doubt -- this only ever WIDENS from
    "ask first" to "act and report," never the reverse, so a false
    negative here just means one more ordinary confirmation message,
    while a false positive means Curant said something to a real
    person without being asked. That asymmetry is exactly why every
    condition here is conservative.
    """
    if is_vip:
        return False
    if sender not in get_auto_reply_handles():
        return False
    if classify_urgency(text) == "urgent":
        return False
    if classify_high_stakes(text):
        return False
    return True


