            "actual content exactly as you always would, this flag doesn't override that."
        )
    if active_provider:
        system_parts.append(
            f"This specific reply is being generated via {active_provider}. If the customer asks which AI "
            "is answering, or which model is currently handling their messages, answer honestly using this — "
            "don't guess or deflect."
        )
    if get_customer_august_tier(config) == "grace":
        system_parts.append(
            "One more reminder on tone, since this matters more than any single feature: reply the way "
            "a sharp, trusted human EA would text back, not the way an AI assistant would. If your draft "
            "reply sounds like it's explaining itself, apologizing pre-emptively, or padding out to "
            "sound thorough, cut that and keep only the actual answer."
        )
        system_parts.append(
            "You're allowed to disagree, and you're allowed to not know something — a real EA does both "
            "regularly, and always deferring or always sounding certain reads as fake, not helpful. This "
            "is separate from the high-stakes decision-handoff rule above (that's about who SHOULD "
            "decide); this is about ordinary conversation. If a plan sounds like a bad idea, say so "
            "directly and briefly, then let them decide what to do with that -- don't just execute it "
            "quietly and don't soften it into mush either. If you're actually not sure about something "
            "factual, say 'I don't know' or 'I'm not sure' plainly instead of guessing with false "
            "confidence or padding around the gap with hedging. Reserve actual agreement for when you "
            "mean it -- don't open with validation ('great question', 'that makes sense') as a reflex "
            "before getting to the substance."
        )
    if extra_instruction:
        system_parts.append(extra_instruction)
    return "\n\n".join(system_parts)


# ── Local front-door layer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A small local model (via Ollama, running on the customer's own Mac)
# sitting IN FRONT OF Claude, never instead of it — Claude stays the
# actual reasoning brain for everything that matters. This layer only
# handles a few narrow, low-stakes jobs before anything reaches the
# network:
#   1. PII redaction — sensitive-looking content (SSNs, credit card
#      numbers) gets masked out of whatever's sent to Claude's API,
#      so the raw value never leaves the device even in transit. Local
#      storage (save_message, memory) keeps the ORIGINAL unredacted
#      text — the point is protecting what crosses the network, not
#      hiding anything from the customer's own machine.
#   2. Triviality short-circuit — a quick "hey" or "thanks" gets a
#      local-generated reply instead of spending a full Claude call on
#      it. Deliberately conservative: any failure to classify (local
#      model unavailable, ambiguous result) defaults to NOT trivial,
#      so this can only ever save a call, never silently downgrade a
#      real request.
#   3. Offline fallback — if Claude's API is unreachable, this tries a
#      degraded local-model reply (clearly flagged as reduced-capability)
#      instead of a hard failure with no answer at all.
#
# HONEST DEPENDENCY, stated plainly: this requires Ollama
# (https://ollama.com) installed and running locally — NOT bundled
# with curant-cli itself. Every function below fails safe (returns
# None / False) if Ollama isn't running, rather than raising — this
# entire layer is an optimization and a privacy improvement, never a
# hard requirement for Curant to work. A customer who never installs
# Ollama gets the exact same behavior as before this layer existed.

OLLAMA_HOST = os.environ.get("CURANT_OLLAMA_HOST", "http://localhost:11434")
LOCAL_MODEL_NAME = os.environ.get("CURANT_LOCAL_MODEL", "qwen3:8b")
LOCAL_MODEL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 8
LOCAL_MODEL_HEALTH_CHECK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 1.5  # short — this is checked on every
                                                   # message, shouldn't add latency
                                                   # when Ollama simply isn't running


def is_local_model_available():
    """Fast health check — short timeout on purpose (see above). Never
    raises; any failure (not installed, not running, wrong port) means
    'not available', not an error."""
    try:
        import requests
        resp = requests.get(f"{OLLAMA_HOST}/api/tags", timeout=LOCAL_MODEL_HEALTH_CHECK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)
        return resp.status_code == 200
    except Exception:
        return False


def _call_ollama(prompt, system="", timeout=LOCAL_MODEL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS):
    """
    Raw call to Ollama's local REST API. Returns the reply text, or
    None on ANY failure — connection refused, timeout, malformed
    response, model not pulled yet. Every caller in this section
    treats None as 'local model unavailable right now' and falls back
    accordingly; nothing here ever raises up to relay().
    """
    try:
        import requests
        resp = requests.post(
            f"{OLLAMA_HOST}/api/chat",
            json={
                "model": LOCAL_MODEL_NAME,
                "messages": ([{"role": "system", "content": system}] if system else [])
                            + [{"role": "user", "content": prompt}],
                "stream": False,
            },
            timeout=timeout,
        )
        resp.raise_for_status()
        data = resp.json()
        return (data.get("message") or {}).get("content", "").strip() or None
    except Exception:
        return None


# ── PII redaction ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Deterministic regex detection, not model-based judgment — same
# principle as _is_sensitive_field's hard block on payment form fields
# elsewhere in this file: a small local model's fuzzy judgment isn't
# reliable enough to be the ONLY line of defense for something this
# security-relevant, so this uses structural pattern matching instead.
#
# Deliberately covers only what can be detected with real confidence:
# SSNs (clean XXX-XX-XXXX format) and credit card numbers (13-19
# digits, Luhn-validated to cut false positives against arbitrary long
# numbers). Bank routing/account numbers are NOT covered — a bare
# 9-digit number has no reliable structural signature distinguishing it
# from any other number, and a noisy pattern that redacts things that
# aren't actually bank numbers would erode trust in this feature faster
# than it protects anything. Better to cover two categories well than
# five categories unreliably.

SSN_PATTERN = re.compile(r"\b\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}\b")
CREDIT_CARD_CANDIDATE_PATTERN = re.compile(r"\b(?:\d[ -]?){13,19}\b")


def _luhn_valid(digit_string):
    digits = [int(d) for d in re.sub(r"[ -]", "", digit_string)]
    checksum = 0
    for i, digit in enumerate(reversed(digits)):
        if i % 2 == 1:
            digit *= 2
            if digit > 9:
                digit -= 9
        checksum += digit
    return checksum % 10 == 0


def redact_pii(text):
    """
    Returns (redacted_text, redaction_count). Only ever called on
    content that's about to leave the device (the API-bound message
    content and the memory-extraction input) — never applied to what's
    saved to local storage, since the whole point is protecting what
    crosses the network, not hiding anything from the customer's own
    machine. Safe on empty/None input.
    """
    if not text:
        return text, 0

    count = 0
    redacted = SSN_PATTERN.sub(lambda m: "[REDACTED_SSN]", text)
    count += len(SSN_PATTERN.findall(text))

    def _replace_card(match):
        nonlocal count
        candidate = match.group(0)
        if not _luhn_valid(candidate):
            return candidate  # fails Luhn check — not actually a valid card number, leave it alone
        count += 1
        return "[REDACTED_CARD_NUMBER]"

    redacted = CREDIT_CARD_CANDIDATE_PATTERN.sub(_replace_card, redacted)
    return redacted, count


# ── Triviality short-circuit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

def _text_prefers_fast_tier(message_text, is_first_message, has_image, urgency, is_high_stakes):
    """
    Task-aware model tier selection for TEXT (relay()'s tier defaults to
    "main" for every text message today, regardless of what the message
    actually needs -- this is the missing half of "smart model
    selection": decide_provider() above already picks WHICH provider
    fits the task, this picks WHICH TIER within that provider).

    Deliberately conservative and purely local (no LLM/network call of
    its own -- classify_message_triviality already spends up to 4s on
    an Ollama round trip just to MAYBE save a cloud call; adding another
    real call here to decide speed would work against the whole point).
    Every signal used is already computed elsewhere in relay() at
    essentially zero marginal cost (classify_urgency, classify_high_stakes,
    is_first_message) or is a single len()/split() on text already in
    memory.

    Refuses fast tier (stays on "main") on any of these, in order of how
    much it would cost to get wrong:
      - has_image: a photo deserves real vision reasoning, not a
        quick/cheap pass.
      - is_first_message: someone's first impression of Curant shouldn't
        be the cut-rate model.
      - urgency == "urgent" / is_high_stakes: exactly the messages this
        codebase already treats with extra care elsewhere (second-
        opinion routing, real-time notification) -- speed is not the
        priority here.
      - anything over a short word count: a message that's actually
        substantive (a real question, a task, a request with detail)
        gets full reasoning quality. This only fires for the kind of
        short, low-stakes, everyday exchange (a quick check-in, a
        one-line follow-up, "what's on my calendar", "did that send")
        where a smaller/faster model answers just as well.

    Every one of these is a false-negative-safe default: getting the
    call "wrong" here only ever means an ordinary message stays on the
    slower/better model, never that something sensitive gets a degraded
    answer -- same asymmetric design as classify_urgency/
    classify_high_stakes above.
    """
    if has_image or is_first_message:
        return False
    if urgency == "urgent" or is_high_stakes:
        return False
    if not message_text or not message_text.strip():
        return False
    return len(message_text.split()) <= 12


def classify_message_triviality(text):
    """
    Conservative by design: returns False (not trivial — use full
    Claude reasoning) on any doubt at all. This can only ever save an
    API call on a genuinely trivial message; it can never cause a real
    request to get a degraded local-only answer, since a wrong "not
    trivial" classification just means the normal path runs anyway.
    """
    if not text or not text.strip():
        return False
    if not is_local_model_available():
        return False
    result = _call_ollama(
        prompt=f"Message: {text}",
        system=(
            "You classify a single incoming message to a personal AI secretary. "
            "Reply with EXACTLY one word: TRIVIAL if this is just a greeting, "
            "acknowledgment, thanks, or similar low-stakes small talk that needs only "
            "a brief friendly reply — SUBSTANTIVE for anything that involves a real "
            "question, task, request, or requires any actual thought. When genuinely "
            "unsure, answer SUBSTANTIVE."
        ),
        timeout=4,  # this is meant to save time, not cost more of it — short budget
    )
    if result is None:
        return False
    return result.strip().upper().startswith("TRIVIAL")


def generate_local_reply(text, persona_name="Curant"):
    """
    Only called after classify_message_triviality already returned
    True, so the local model is confirmed available — but still handles
    a None result gracefully (a race between the two calls, or Ollama
    going down in between) rather than ever returning nothing.
    """
    reply = _call_ollama(
        prompt=text,
        system=(f"You are {persona_name}, a personal AI secretary. Reply briefly and "
                f"warmly to this short, low-stakes message — a sentence or two at most."),
    )
    return reply or "Got it!"


def generate_offline_reply(text, persona_name="Curant"):
    """
    Called only when the real Claude call already failed (see relay()).
    Returns None if the local model ISN'T available either — in that
    case relay() falls back to its original hard-failure behavior,
    since there's genuinely nothing left to try.
    """
    if not is_local_model_available():
        return None
    return _call_ollama(
        prompt=text,
        system=(
            f"You are {persona_name}, a personal AI secretary, currently running in a "
            f"reduced OFFLINE mode because the main connection is unreachable. Be "
            f"upfront about that — you may not have full context or your usual "
            f"capabilities right now. Help as best you can with just what's in this "
            f"message, and mention that full service will resume once the connection "
            f"is back."
        ),
    )


def _get_second_opinion(message_text, draft_reply, config, provider, api_key):
    """
    Grace-exclusive. For messages that look genuinely high-stakes
    (classify_high_stakes), runs a SEPARATE review call against the
    draft reply before it goes out -- a second, independently-prompted
    pass asking "is there anything risky, inaccurate, or worth a human
    double-checking here?" rather than just trusting the first pass.
    This is not a second full generation competing with the first
    (that would just be two guesses, not a check) -- it's specifically
    a critique-the-draft call, which is a different and more useful
    question to ask a model than "answer this again."

    Returns None on any error, no API key, non-Grace tier, or when the
    reviewer finds nothing worth flagging -- always fails closed to "no
    note," never blocks or delays the underlying reply, which is
    already generated and saved by the time this runs.
    """
    if get_customer_august_tier(config) != "grace":
        return None
    if not api_key:
        return None
    try:
        extra = (
            "You are doing a SECOND, independent review of a draft reply before it's sent -- not "
            "writing a new reply yourself. Here is the original message:\n"
            f"\"{message_text}\"\n\n"
            "Here is the draft reply about to be sent:\n"
            f"\"{draft_reply}\"\n\n"
            "This message looked high-stakes (financial, legal, medical, or a major life/relationship "
            "decision). Check the draft for anything risky, inaccurate, overconfident, or that a "
            "prudent person would want a second pair of eyes on before acting on it -- e.g. legal or "
            "medical specifics stated as fact rather than caveated, financial figures or commitments "
            "that could be wrong, irreversible actions treated too casually.\n\n"
            "If the draft is genuinely fine as-is, respond with exactly: "
            '{"flagged": false}\n'
            "If there's something worth flagging, respond with a SHORT (one or two sentence) plain-"
            "language caution, not a rewrite: "
            '{"flagged": true, "note": "..."}\n\n'
            "Respond with ONLY that JSON object, nothing else."
        )
        system_prompt = build_system_prompt(config, [], [], "", extra_instruction=extra)
        raw = call_llm(
            provider, api_key, system_prompt,
            messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Review the draft reply now."}],
            max_tokens=200, tier="fast",
        ).strip()
        if raw.startswith("```"):
            raw = raw.strip("`")
            if raw.startswith("json"):
                raw = raw[4:]
        result = json.loads(raw)
        if not result.get("flagged"):
            return None
        note = (result.get("note") or "").strip()
        return note or None
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[second opinion] review failed (non-fatal): {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return None


# ── Personalization phase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A short, scripted onboarding Q&A that kicks in when the customer
# explicitly asks Curant to get to know them. Deliberately NOT an LLM
# call at any point -- trigger detection is a plain keyword/regex match,
# questions are canned text, and every answer is saved straight into
# the local `memories` table via save_memory(), exactly the same as
# anything else Curant learns in normal conversation (so it's
# immediately visible in memories.md and immediately part of future
# system prompts -- see the memories.md feature). Session progress
# lives in config.json under "personalization", the same place
# quiet_hours/travel_mode/etc. already live -- a single small
# in-progress-or-not blob, not something that needs its own table.

PERSONALIZATION_TRIGGER_PATTERNS = [
    re.compile(p, re.IGNORECASE) for p in [
        r"\blearn (more )?about me\b",
        r"\bget to know me\b",
        r"\bknow (more )?about me\b",
        r"\blearn who i am\b",
        r"\bknow who i am\b",
        r"\bget to know who i am\b",
        r"\bpersonalize (yourself|you|this|curant)\b",
        r"\bpersonalization\b",
    ]
]

# Ordered on purpose: name first (so later questions/replies can eventually
# use it), then the lower-stakes factual ones, ending on the open-ended one
# that benefits from whatever rapport the earlier answers built.
PERSONALIZATION_QUESTIONS = [
    {
        "question": "I'd love to get to know you better. First — what should I call you?",
        "memory_template": "Prefers to be called {answer}.",
    },
    {
        "question": "How old are you (or whatever you're comfortable sharing — a decade works fine too)?",
        "memory_template": "Age / life stage: {answer}.",
    },
    {
        "question": "What do you do for work?",
        "memory_template": "Occupation: {answer}.",
    },
    {
        "question": "Where are you based — city and/or timezone helps most?",
        "memory_template": "Based in: {answer}.",
    },
    {
        "question": "Last one — anything about your day-to-day worth knowing? Family, hobbies, what "
                     "a typical week looks like, anything like that.",
        "memory_template": "{answer}",
    },
]

_PERSONALIZATION_SKIP_WORDS = {
    "skip", "pass", "n/a", "na", "no", "nah", "prefer not to say",
    "rather not say", "rather not", "next", "none",
}

_PERSONALIZATION_CANCEL_PATTERN = re.compile(
    r"\b(stop|cancel|nevermind|never mind|quit)\b", re.IGNORECASE
)


def _is_personalization_trigger(message_text):
    if not message_text:
        return False
    return any(p.search(message_text) for p in PERSONALIZATION_TRIGGER_PATTERNS)


def _is_personalization_skip(answer_text):
    return (answer_text or "").strip().lower().rstrip(".!") in _PERSONALIZATION_SKIP_WORDS


def start_personalization_session(config):
    """
    Begins the scripted Q&A and persists progress into config.json so it
    survives across relay() calls -- each incoming text is its own
    process, so state has to live on disk, not in memory. Returns the
    first question to send back as the reply.
    """
    config["personalization"] = {"active": True, "question_index": 0}
    save_config(config)
    return PERSONALIZATION_QUESTIONS[0]["question"]


def continue_personalization_session(config, message_text):
    """
    Called when a personalization session is already active and a new
    message just arrived -- treats it as the answer to the current
    question (unless it looks like a skip or a cancel), saves it as a
    real memory, and either asks the next question or wraps up.
    Returns the reply text to send back.
    """
    state = config.get("personalization") or {}
    idx = state.get("question_index", 0)

    if _PERSONALIZATION_CANCEL_PATTERN.search(message_text or ""):
        config["personalization"] = {"active": False, "question_index": 0}
        save_config(config)
        return "No problem — we can pick this back up anytime, just say \"learn about me\"."

    if idx >= len(PERSONALIZATION_QUESTIONS):
        # Shouldn't normally happen (the session should already have ended),
        # but fail safe instead of indexing out of range.
        config["personalization"] = {"active": False, "question_index": 0}
        save_config(config)
        return "All set — thanks for sharing that."

    if not _is_personalization_skip(message_text):
        template = PERSONALIZATION_QUESTIONS[idx]["memory_template"]
        save_memory(template.format(answer=message_text.strip()))

    next_idx = idx + 1
    if next_idx >= len(PERSONALIZATION_QUESTIONS):
        config["personalization"] = {"active": False, "question_index": 0}
        save_config(config)
        return ("That's everything — thanks for sharing. I'll keep this in mind going forward. "
                "You can see everything I've saved anytime with `curant-cli memories`, or just "
                "tell me more whenever something comes to mind.")

    config["personalization"] = {"active": True, "question_index": next_idx}
    save_config(config)
    return PERSONALIZATION_QUESTIONS[next_idx]["question"]


def relay(message_text, context="", image_path=None, apple_id=None, tier="main", voice_mode=False):
    """
    Handle an incoming message entirely locally: verify entitlement (using
    a cached status check, not a live call for every message), build the
    system prompt from local persona/instructions/memories/important
    people, call whichever model provider is configured with the
    customer's own API key, save the exchange locally, and kick off
    background memory extraction. If image_path is given, the model
    actually sees the image (a real vision content block, provider-
    appropriate) rather than the watcher trying to describe it first.

    Always prints structured JSON so curant-watcher.py's json.loads() never
    breaks, matching the previous client/server contract.

    tier defaults to "main" (the full model) for normal text relay. Callers
    that need lower latency more than top reasoning quality — currently
    just the experimental FaceTime answerer, where a caller is waiting in
    real time — can pass tier="fast" to use each provider's quicker model.

    voice_mode=True (only ever passed by the FaceTime answerer, via the
    CLI's --voice flag) appends a spoken-style instruction to the system
    prompt and caps reply length lower than text's default -- a live
    caller heard a real reply read out loud that took 14 seconds just to
    say "I don't have access to your local clock..." formatted like a
    written paragraph. Text and voice share the exact same persona/
    memory/instructions; only the delivery-format instruction and the
    token cap differ here, on purpose -- see build_system_prompt's
    extra_instruction param.
    """
    config = load_config()
    key = config.get("license_key")
    if not key and not DEV_UNLICENSED:
        print(json.dumps({"reply": None, "error": "not_activated"}))
        sys.exit(1)
    if DEV_UNLICENSED:
        _warn_dev_unlicensed()
        # Normal activation calls init_local_db() once; the bypass skips
        # activation entirely, so do it here instead. Idempotent (CREATE
        # TABLE IF NOT EXISTS throughout) — safe to call on every relay().
        init_local_db()

    _last_generated_files.clear()  # this turn's generations only, not leftover from a prior call

    global _current_apple_id
    _current_apple_id = apple_id

    provider = get_provider(config)
    api_key = get_api_key(config, provider)
    if not api_key:
        report_error("no_api_key_set", "curant-cli")
        print(json.dumps({"reply": None, "error": "no_api_key_set"}))
        sys.exit(1)

    if DEV_UNLICENSED:
        active = True  # entitlement bypass (CURANT_DEV_UNLICENSED); warned above
    else:
        config, active = refresh_status_if_stale(config)
    if not active:
        report_error("inactive_or_invalid_license", "curant-cli")
        print(json.dumps({"reply": None, "error": "inactive_or_invalid_license"}))
        sys.exit(1)

    # Personalization phase -- checked before the triviality short-circuit
    # below, since "learn about me" would otherwise get handed to the tiny
    # local model as if it were ordinary small talk, with no session/memory
    # logic at all. Skipped entirely in voice_mode -- a live caller getting
    # walked through five sequential questions read aloud one at a time
    # isn't a good phone experience; personalization is a text-only flow.
    # See start_personalization_session / continue_personalization_session.
    if not voice_mode:
        personalization_state = config.get("personalization") or {}
        if personalization_state.get("active"):
            reply_text = continue_personalization_session(config, message_text)
            save_message("user", message_text, urgency="normal")
            save_message("assistant", reply_text, provider="local")
            print(json.dumps({
                "reply": reply_text,
                "reply_format": config.get("reply_format", "text"),
                "voice_tier": config.get("voice_tier", "standard"),
                "attachment_path": None,
            }))
            return
        elif not image_path and _is_personalization_trigger(message_text):
            reply_text = start_personalization_session(config)
            save_message("user", message_text, urgency="normal")
            save_message("assistant", reply_text, provider="local")
            print(json.dumps({
                "reply": reply_text,
                "reply_format": config.get("reply_format", "text"),
                "voice_tier": config.get("voice_tier", "standard"),
                "attachment_path": None,
            }))
            return

    # Local front-door layer, step 1: triviality short-circuit. Only
    # attempted for plain text (an image always deserves real reasoning
    # to actually look at it) and only ever SAVES a Claude call — never
    # skips one on uncertain ground, since classify_message_triviality
    # defaults to False on any doubt at all.
    #
    # SKIPPED entirely in voice_mode -- real live-call latency
    # diagnosis: a caller reported a "long delay between hearing
    # something and answering," and this step is a big part of it. It
    # runs an Ollama call (up to a 4s timeout) BEFORE the real reply
    # path even starts, purely to maybe save a cloud call -- a trade
    # that makes sense for a text message someone reads whenever, but
    # is pure added latency in front of a live caller who's waiting on
    # the line right now, whether or not it ends up saving anything.
    if not voice_mode and not image_path and classify_message_triviality(message_text):
        local_reply = generate_local_reply(message_text, persona_name=config.get("persona", "Curant").title())
        save_message("user", message_text, urgency="normal")
        save_message("assistant", local_reply, provider="local")
        print(json.dumps({
            "reply": local_reply,
            "reply_format": config.get("reply_format", "text"),
            "voice_tier": config.get("voice_tier", "standard"),
            "attachment_path": None,
        }))
        return

    memories = get_memories()
    people = get_important_people()

    # Multi-provider routing: if more than one provider has a key
    # configured, let this specific message be answered by whichever one
    # actually suits it — a one-off request in the message itself, an
    # existing standing rule, or (falling back) a judgment call about
    # relative model strengths. Skipped entirely with only one provider
    # configured, so this never adds cost/latency to the common case.
    #
    # ALSO skipped entirely in voice_mode, regardless of how many
    # providers are configured -- same latency reasoning as above:
    # decide_provider() is a full extra LLM call (tier="fast", but
    # still a real network round trip) made JUST to decide who answers,
    # before the actual reply generation call even starts. For a live
    # caller that's a second sequential model call stacked in front of
    # the one that actually produces their answer -- worth it for text
    # (no one's waiting in real time), not worth it for a call. Voice
    # always uses the person's default configured provider.
    if voice_mode:
        eligible, routed_provider, routing_reason, new_rule_saved = [provider], provider, None, False
    else:
        eligible = get_eligible_providers(config)
        routed_provider, routing_reason, new_rule_saved = decide_provider(
            message_text, config, provider, eligible
        )
    if routed_provider != provider:
        # Routing to a different provider than "the" configured one means
        # a different API key too.
        routed_api_key = get_api_key(config, routed_provider)
        if routed_api_key:
            provider = routed_provider
            api_key = routed_api_key

    history = get_history(limit=_message_history_keep(config))
    is_first_message = len(history) == 0
    urgency = classify_urgency(message_text)
    # Computed here (once, before the model call) rather than only at the
    # existing second-opinion check further down, so both the new tier
    # decision below AND that check share one result instead of running
    # this cheap local keyword check twice.
    is_high_stakes_msg = classify_high_stakes(message_text)

    # Smart model tier selection, text side (voice already gets tier="fast"
    # unconditionally from its caller -- see relay()'s docstring). See
    # _text_prefers_fast_tier's docstring for the full reasoning. Only
    # ever DOWNGRADES an explicit tier="main" call to "fast" -- never
    # overrides a caller that already asked for tier="fast" or anything
    # else, and never touches voice_mode (which has its own, separate,
    # already-latency-tuned path).
    if not voice_mode and tier == "main" and _text_prefers_fast_tier(
        message_text, is_first_message, bool(image_path), urgency, is_high_stakes_msg
    ):
        tier = "fast"

    voice_instruction = (
        "This message came in through a live FaceTime voice call, not a text -- your reply will be "
        "read aloud by text-to-speech to someone waiting on the line right now, not read silently as "
        "text. Talk the way a person actually talks on the phone: short sentences, contractions, no "
        "markdown, no bullet points or numbered lists, no headers, nothing that only makes sense "
        "written down. Keep it brief -- a couple of sentences, not a paragraph -- and get to the point "
        "fast; you can always say more if they ask a follow-up. If you'd normally write a list, say the "
        "items as a spoken sentence instead (\"a few options: X, Y, or Z\")."
    ) if voice_mode else None

    # Grace-exclusive relationship-state tone shaping (Step 3 of the
    # "make Curant more human" work) -- skipped in voice_mode, same
    # latency reasoning as decide_provider above: this can trigger a
    # real extra LLM call (only when the cache is stale, see
    # RELATIONSHIP_STATE_MAX_AGE_HOURS), and a live caller shouldn't
    # wait on that. Text replies aren't time-critical the same way.
    relationship_state = None if voice_mode else get_relationship_state(config, provider, api_key)

    system_prompt = build_system_prompt(
        config, memories, people, context, extra_instruction=voice_instruction,
        active_provider=provider, is_first_message=is_first_message, urgency=urgency,
        relationship_state=relationship_state,
    )

    # Local front-door layer, step 2: PII redaction. Applied ONLY to what
    # actually leaves the device — the content sent to Claude below, and
    # (further down) what's passed into memory extraction, which is
    # itself a real API call and would otherwise defeat the point of
    # redacting here at all. Local storage (save_message, a few lines
    # down) deliberately keeps the ORIGINAL message_text — nothing about
    # this feature is about hiding anything from the customer's own
    # machine, only about what crosses the network.
    redacted_message_text, pii_redaction_count = redact_pii(message_text)
    if pii_redaction_count:
        print(f"[front-door] redacted {pii_redaction_count} PII match(es) before sending to {provider}",
              file=sys.stderr)

    if image_path:
        user_content = build_image_message_content(provider, image_path, redacted_message_text)
    else:
        user_content = redacted_message_text
    history.append({"role": "user", "content": user_content})

    # Tool support: log_capability_gap is always available; any
    # configured MCP servers add whatever they expose. Both providers'
    # tool-calling loop handle this the same way from here on.
    available_tools = get_all_available_tools(config)

    # Voice replies are spoken aloud in real time to someone waiting on
    # the line, so they're capped shorter than text's 1000-token default
    # -- LOWERED further, 300 -> 150 (2026-08-08, "make transcription and
    # response faster"), then 150 -> 100 (this change, "cut call latency
    # ~0.5s" request): fewer tokens the model has to generate is a
    # direct, proportional latency win on top of the brevity instruction
    # (which asks nicely but doesn't bound worst case) -- 100 tokens is
    # still a genuine sentence or two, not a single word, but this is
    # getting close to the floor of "still sounds like a real answer."
    # NOT independently measured against a live call yet (this sandbox
    # has no network access to actually time a real model call) -- the
    # mechanism (fewer tokens = proportionally less generation time) is
    # well-established, but the exact wall-clock savings need confirming
    # on a real call using the per-turn timing the FaceTime answerer
    # already prints. Real tradeoff, not hidden: a reply that genuinely
    # needs more room gets cut off mid-thought instead of finishing --
    # raise this back up (100 -> 150 -> 300) if that starts happening in
    # real calls.
    reply_max_tokens = 100 if voice_mode else 1000
    try:
        if available_tools:
            reply_text = call_llm_with_tools(
                provider, api_key, system_prompt, history, available_tools, available_tools,
                max_tokens=reply_max_tokens, tier=tier,
            )
        else:
            reply_text = call_llm(provider, api_key, system_prompt, history, max_tokens=reply_max_tokens, tier=tier)
    except AllModelsExhaustedError as e:
        # Every model in this provider's fallback chain hit a rate/quota
        # limit (see call_llm/_model_fallback_chain) — deliberately NOT
        # routed through generate_offline_reply() below: this isn't a
        # connectivity problem a local model can stand in for, it's "you
        # are out of free-tier quota everywhere right now," and the
        # honest reply is a fixed, code-written notice saying so — not
        # an LLM-generated answer (there's no quota left to generate one
        # with) and not a misleading offline reply that looks like a
        # normal answer.
        reply_text = (
            "(Automatic notice — not a generated reply) I've hit the free-tier rate/quota limit "
            "on every model I have available right now, so I can't generate a real response to "
            "this yet. This usually clears itself within a few minutes — try sending this again "
            "shortly, or ask about switching to a paid tier / another provider if this keeps "
            "happening."
        )
        provider = "quota_exhausted_notice"
        print(f"[quota] all models exhausted for this message: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        report_error("all_models_exhausted", "curant-cli")
    except Exception as e:
        # Local front-door layer, step 3: offline fallback. Only
        # attempted after the real call has already failed — this never
        # substitutes for Claude when Claude is actually reachable, it
        # only fills the gap between "the connection is down" and
        # "total silence." generate_offline_reply returns None if the
        # local model isn't available either, in which case this falls
        # through to the original hard-failure behavior unchanged.
        offline_reply = generate_offline_reply(
            redacted_message_text, persona_name=config.get("persona", "Curant").title()
        )
        if offline_reply:
            reply_text = offline_reply
            provider = "local_offline"
            report_error("llm_call_failed", "curant-cli")  # still worth knowing the real call failed
        else:
            report_error("llm_call_failed", "curant-cli")
            print(json.dumps({"reply": f"Couldn't reach {provider} on your account: {e}", "error": "llm_call_failed"}))
            sys.exit(1)

    # Grace-exclusive second-opinion routing: for messages that look
    # genuinely high-stakes, run an independent review pass on the draft
    # reply before it's saved/returned. Skipped for voice (latency-
    # sensitive, same reasoning as the other voice_mode skips above) and
    # for the two non-generated fallback replies (quota-exhausted notice,
    # offline local reply) -- there's no real model output to review in
    # either of those cases.
    if not voice_mode and provider not in ("quota_exhausted_notice", "local_offline") and is_high_stakes_msg:
        second_opinion_note = _get_second_opinion(message_text, reply_text, config, provider, api_key)
        if second_opinion_note:
            reply_text = f"{reply_text}\n\n(Second opinion: {second_opinion_note})"

    user_history_text = message_text if message_text else ("[sent an image]" if image_path else message_text)
    save_message("user", user_history_text, urgency=urgency)
    save_message("assistant", reply_text, provider=provider)

    # Track usage as a count only — reported to the server on the next
    # periodic status check, never per-message.
    config = load_config()
    config["pending_usage_count"] = config.get("pending_usage_count", 0) + 1
    save_config(config)

    # Memory extraction — kept synchronous but cheap (the "fast" tier
    # model); this process exits right after, so a background thread
    # would risk being killed before it finishes. A few hundred ms here
    # is an acceptable tradeoff for memory reliably actually happening.
    # Uses the REDACTED text, not user_history_text — this is a real API
    # call too, and would otherwise leak whatever the front-door layer
    # just redacted from the main conversation turn.
    #
    # SKIPPED in voice_mode. Real live-call latency diagnosis (caller
    # reported "a long delay between hearing something and answering"):
    # this is a THIRD sequential LLM call this function was making
    # per turn, and it runs BEFORE the JSON reply below is printed —
    # subprocess.run(capture_output=True) in the FaceTime answerer's
    # get_reply() doesn't return until this whole process exits, not
    # just until the reply is generated, so the caller was sitting
    # through this extra call's full round trip on every single turn
    # even though it's about remembering the conversation, not
    # answering it. "A few hundred ms" per the comment above was true
    # for a text relay's real-world timing but not for what was
    # actually observed live on calls. Tradeoff accepted: call
    # conversations currently don't feed the memory system the way
    # texts do — revisit with a true fire-and-forget background
    # process (detached child, not a thread) if that's ever wanted.
    if not voice_mode:
        update_memories_from_exchange(provider, api_key, memories, redacted_message_text, reply_text)

    # Generated files sit encrypted on disk (see _save_generated_bytes) —
    # decrypt to a short-lived temp file only at the moment of actually
    # handing off to the watcher for delivery. curant-watcher.py deletes
    # this temp copy immediately after sending; the long-term encrypted
    # copy in ~/.curant/generated/ is what prune_generated_files() manages.
    attachment_path = None
    if _last_generated_files:
        try:
            encrypted_path = _last_generated_files[-1]
            plaintext_bytes = decrypt_generated_file(encrypted_path)
            temp_ext = os.path.basename(encrypted_path).split(".")[-2]  # e.g. "png" from "png_abc.png.enc"
            fd, attachment_path = tempfile.mkstemp(suffix=f".{temp_ext}")
            with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
                f.write(plaintext_bytes)
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"Could not decrypt generated file for delivery (non-fatal): {e}", file=sys.stderr)

    print(json.dumps({
        "reply": reply_text,
        "reply_format": config.get("reply_format", "text"),
        "voice_tier": config.get("voice_tier", "standard"),
        "attachment_path": attachment_path,
    }))


def _looks_like_spam(handle, text):
    """Lightweight, explainable heuristic for obvious marketing/automated
    spam -- deliberately conservative (false negatives are fine, a
    missed spam text just falls through to the normal "ask the customer"
    screening path; false POSITIVES are the expensive mistake here,
    since a match here auto-marks the handle as spam and future real
    messages from them go silently dropped). Two real, well-known
    signals, not guesses:
      1. Common carrier/marketing spam phrasing (opt-out language, prize/
         urgency bait) -- the same kind of boilerplate every SMS spam
         filter looks for.
      2. US short-code senders (5-6 digit numeric handles) -- these are
         registered exclusively for bulk/automated SMS (marketing,
         verification codes, alerts), never a real person's personal
         number, so a message FROM one is essentially never someone the
         customer needs to personally screen.
    """
    text_lower = (text or "").lower()
    spam_phrases = (
        "reply stop", "text stop to", "unsubscribe", "you've won", "you have won",
        "claim your prize", "verification code", "limited time offer", "act now",
        "call now", "free gift", "no cost to you", "% off today only", "msg & data rates",
    )
    if any(p in text_lower for p in spam_phrases):
        return True
    digits = (handle or "").lstrip("+")
    if digits.isdigit() and len(digits) in (5, 6):
        return True
    return False


def _draft_suggested_reply(sender, text, resolved_name, person):
    """
    Grace-exclusive. Drafts a plausible reply to a third party's
    message for the customer to review/edit/send themselves --
    screen_message() NEVER sends this on its own; it's included in the
    notify_owner notice purely as a starting point. Returns None (no
    draft) for anyone below Grace, on any error, or if the customer
    hasn't got an API key configured -- always fails closed to "no
    draft" rather than blocking the underlying notify_owner notice,
    which always goes out regardless of whether this succeeds.
    """
    config = load_config()
    if get_customer_august_tier(config) != "grace":
        return None
    provider = get_provider(config)
    api_key = get_api_key(config, provider)
    if not api_key:
        return None
    try:
        who_desc = resolved_name or "this person"
        relationship_note = ""
        if person and (person.get("relationship") or person.get("role")):
            relationship_note = f" ({person.get('relationship') or person.get('role')})"
        extra = (
            f"A third party ({who_desc}{relationship_note}) just texted the customer this: "
            f"\"{text}\"\n\n"
            "Draft a short, natural reply the CUSTOMER could send back, in the customer's own "
            "voice (not as Curant, not mentioning you're an AI) -- this is only ever a "
            "suggestion for them to review, edit, or discard, never sent automatically. Keep it "
            "brief and natural, matching how a real text reply would actually sound.\n\n"
            'Respond with ONLY a JSON object: {"draft": "..."}.'
        )
        system_prompt = build_system_prompt(config, [], [], "", extra_instruction=extra)
        raw = call_llm(
            provider, api_key, system_prompt,
            messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Draft the suggested reply."}],
            max_tokens=200, tier="fast",
        ).strip()
        if raw.startswith("```"):
            raw = raw.strip("`")
            if raw.startswith("json"):
                raw = raw[4:]
        result = json.loads(raw)
        draft = (result.get("draft") or "").strip()
        return draft or None
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[suggested reply] draft failed (non-fatal): {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return None


def screen_message(sender, text, contact_name=None):
    """
    Decides what curant-watcher.py should do with an incoming text from
    someone who is NOT the customer (a third party) -- the customer's own
    messages always go straight through relay() unchanged; this is only
    reached when CURANT_TEXT_ACCESS_MODE=open lets other people's texts
    through the door at all. See curant-watcher.py's text/call
    access-mode split for the incident this whole screening layer exists
    to prevent: a third party never gets auto-replied to here, ever --
    the only two outcomes are "silently ignore" (spam) or "tell the
    customer, let THEM decide" (everyone else, known or not). Actually
    replying to a third party on the customer's behalf isn't built yet;
    see the comment at this function's call site in curant-watcher.py.

    Always prints exactly one JSON object to stdout:
      {"action": "ignore", "reason": "..."}
      {"action": "notify_owner", "notice": "...", "sender": "...", "resolved_name": "..." or null, "vip": true/false}
      {"action": "auto_reply", "reply": "...", "notice": "...", "sender": "...", "resolved_name": "..." or null}

    VIP escalation (Grace-exclusive -- see get_vip_handles/mark_vip_cmd):
    a VIP sender's message is NEVER auto-ignored as spam, even if it
    happens to trip the heuristic spam-phrase matcher below, and the
    notice text is explicitly flagged so it stands out from an ordinary
    unknown-sender notice. This function itself doesn't check tier --
    that's enforced at the point VIP status can be SET (mark_vip_cmd) and
    at the point a VIP's message is even allowed to reach here at all in
    'approved' access mode (curant-watcher.py's fetch_new_messages,
    gated on get_vip_handles which is only ever non-empty for Grace
    customers in practice, since mark_vip_cmd refuses to set it otherwise).

    Bounded discretion (available on every tier including basic, opt-in
    -- see add_auto_reply_contact_cmd/_is_safe_to_autoreply): for a specific,
    customer-approved contact, a message that is ALSO judged routine
    (not urgent, not high-stakes, sender not VIP) gets ACTUALLY replied
    to on the customer's behalf, rather than only ever drafted and held
    for permission -- curant-watcher.py sends the reply and separately
    tells the customer it happened, after the fact, never before. This
    is the one deliberate exception to this function's historical
    "never actually replies to a third party" behavior, and it only
    ever applies to senders the customer explicitly opted in one at a
    time -- see _is_safe_to_autoreply for the full, conservative gate.
    """
    init_local_db()

    if is_spam_handle(sender):
        print(json.dumps({"action": "ignore", "reason": "marked as spam"}))
        return

    person = get_person_by_handle(sender)
    is_vip = bool(person and person.get("is_vip"))

    if not is_vip and person is None and _looks_like_spam(sender, text):
        set_person_spam(sender, is_spam=True)
        print(json.dumps({"action": "ignore", "reason": "heuristic spam match — marked spam so it won't ask again"}))
        return

    resolved_name = contact_name or (person.get("name") if person else None) or lookup_macos_contact(sender)
    preview = (text or "").strip()
    if len(preview) > 200:
        preview = preview[:200] + "…"
    who = f"{resolved_name} ({sender})" if resolved_name else f"an unknown number ({sender})"
    known_note = ""
    if person and (person.get("relationship") or person.get("role")):
        known_note = f" You've got them saved as: {person.get('relationship') or ''} {person.get('role') or ''}.".strip()

    urgency = classify_urgency(preview) if preview else None
    urgency_note = " (This message also reads as urgent.)" if urgency == "urgent" else ""

    suggested_reply = _draft_suggested_reply(sender, text, resolved_name, person) if preview else None

    if suggested_reply and _is_safe_to_autoreply(sender, preview, is_vip):
        fyi_notice = (
            f"FYI -- {who} texted \"{preview}\" and I went ahead and replied for them since that's "
            f"routine and you've told me it's fine to just handle their messages: \"{suggested_reply}\"\n\n"
            f"Say the word if you'd rather I hadn't, or if you want to take auto-reply off for them."
        )
        print(json.dumps({
            "action": "auto_reply", "reply": suggested_reply, "notice": fyi_notice,
            "sender": sender, "resolved_name": resolved_name,
        }))
        return

    draft_block = f"\n\nSuggested reply (just my draft -- I never send this on my own): \"{suggested_reply}\"" if suggested_reply else ""

    if is_vip:
        notice = (
            f"🚨 VIP CONTACT: {who} just texted.{known_note}{urgency_note}\n\n"
            f"Their message: \"{preview}\"{draft_block}\n\n"
            f"Want me to send that, something else, or nothing at all?"
        )
    else:
        notice = (
            f"You got a text from {who} that I haven't replied to — I only ever text people back if "
            f"it's you, or you've told me to.{known_note}{urgency_note}\n\nTheir message: \"{preview}\"{draft_block}\n\n"
            f"Want me to send that, something else, or nothing at all? (Or tell me to mark them as spam "
            f"if this isn't someone you know.)"
        )
    print(json.dumps({
        "action": "notify_owner", "notice": notice, "sender": sender,
        "resolved_name": resolved_name, "vip": is_vip, "suggested_reply": suggested_reply,
    }))


def proactive_check(context):
    """
    Decide locally whether a proactive (unprompted) check-in is warranted
    right now, using the same local persona/memory/instructions context.
    Deliberately conservative — most days should conclude nothing is worth
    sending, and any error fails closed (nothing sent) rather than risking
    a spurious message.
    """
    config = load_config()
    key = config.get("license_key")
    if not key:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False, "error": "not_activated"}))
        sys.exit(1)

    if not config.get("proactivity_enabled"):
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))
        return

    provider = get_provider(config)
    api_key = get_api_key(config, provider)
    if not api_key:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False, "error": "no_api_key_set"}))
        return

    config, active = refresh_status_if_stale(config)
    if not active:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False, "error": "inactive_or_invalid_license"}))
        return

    if not context:
        # Nothing to reason about — don't manufacture a check-in from thin air.
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))
        return

    memories = get_memories()
    people = get_important_people()
    extra = (
        "You are deciding whether to proactively message this person right now, "
        "unprompted — they haven't texted you. Only do this if something in their "
        "calendar/reminders genuinely warrants a heads-up (conflicts, something "
        "easy to forget, a change worth flagging). Most days there is nothing worth "
        "sending, and that is the correct, expected outcome — do not invent a reason "
        "to reach out just to seem useful.\n\n"
        f"Today's live context:\n{context}\n\n"
        'Respond with ONLY a JSON object: {"should_send": true/false, "message": "..."} '
        '— message should be empty string if should_send is false.'
    )
    system_prompt = build_system_prompt(config, memories, people, "", extra_instruction=extra)

    try:
        raw = call_llm(
            provider, api_key, system_prompt,
            messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Decide whether to send a proactive check-in now."}],
            max_tokens=300, tier="main",
        ).strip()
        if raw.startswith("```"):
            raw = raw.strip("`")
            if raw.startswith("json"):
                raw = raw[4:]
        decision = json.loads(raw)
    except Exception as e:
        # Fail closed — a broken proactivity check should never spam the
        # customer, it should just silently not send anything.
        print(f"Proactive check failed (non-fatal, not sending): {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))
        return

    if decision.get("should_send") and decision.get("message"):
        save_message("assistant", decision["message"])
        print(json.dumps({
            "should_send": True,
            "message": decision["message"],
            "reply_format": config.get("reply_format", "text"),
            "voice_tier": config.get("voice_tier", "standard"),
        }))
    else:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))


EMAIL_SERVER_NAME_HINTS = ("gmail", "email", "outlook", "mail")
ORDER_SEARCH_TOOL_NAME_HINTS = ("search",)  # prefer an actual query-capable tool over a
                                              # blind "list everything" one for this use case
ORDER_SEARCH_PARAM_NAME_HINTS = ("query", "q", "search", "term", "text")


def _pick_query_param_name(input_schema):
    """Best-effort guess at which property name a given MCP tool's
    input_schema expects the search string under -- server authors
    don't agree on this (query/q/search/term/text all show up in the
    wild), and this file has no fixed contract with whatever email MCP
    server the customer happened to connect. Falls back to the first
    required string property, then the first property at all, then
    None (meaning: don't guess, don't call it) if the schema is empty
    or unreadable."""
    try:
        props = (input_schema or {}).get("properties") or {}
        for hint in ORDER_SEARCH_PARAM_NAME_HINTS:
            if hint in props:
                return hint
        required = (input_schema or {}).get("required") or []
        for name in required:
            if props.get(name, {}).get("type") == "string":
                return name
        return next(iter(props), None)
    except Exception:
        return None


def search_order_status_email(query, config):
    """
    Grace-exclusive, best-effort (Step: closing the Amazon-order-status
    capability gap logged via log_capability_gap). Deliberately does
    NOT attempt to log into Amazon or any retailer -- fill_and_submit_form
    already refuses login pages on its own, and this file has no
    business storing a customer's retailer credentials to work around
    that. What's actually buildable and safe: Amazon (and most
    retailers) email order confirmations and shipping updates, so if
    the customer has an email-like MCP server connected, this searches
    it for the query and returns a short raw excerpt for the model to
    summarize -- same read-only, best-effort pattern as
    _get_email_summary_for_briefing, just query-driven instead of a
    blind inbox listing, and callable mid-conversation instead of only
    for the daily digest.

    Returns a plain string on success. Returns a clear "couldn't find
    a connected email tool" string (not None) on failure, so the model
    always has something honest to tell the customer rather than
    silently going quiet -- this is called directly as a tool result,
    not folded into a larger digest the way the briefing version is.
    """
    try:
        for server in get_mcp_servers(enabled_only=True):
            name_lower = (server.get("name") or "").lower()
            if not any(hint in name_lower for hint in EMAIL_SERVER_NAME_HINTS):
                continue
            tools = _get_server_tools_cached(server)
            candidate = next(
                (t for t in tools if any(hint in t["name"].lower() for hint in ORDER_SEARCH_TOOL_NAME_HINTS)),
                None,
            ) or next(
                (t for t in tools if any(hint in t["name"].lower() for hint in EMAIL_TOOL_NAME_HINTS)),
                None,
            )
            if not candidate:
                continue
            param_name = _pick_query_param_name(candidate.get("input_schema"))
            arguments = {param_name: query} if param_name else {}
            result = call_mcp_tool(server, candidate["name"], arguments)
            if not result or (isinstance(result, str) and result.startswith("[Error")):
                continue
            text = result if isinstance(result, str) else json.dumps(result)
            return text[:2000]
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[order status search] email lookup failed (non-fatal): {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    return ("No connected email tool could be searched for this -- either nothing's connected, "
            "or the search didn't turn up a matching order/shipping email.")



EMAIL_TOOL_NAME_HINTS = ("list", "search", "unread", "messages", "inbox")


def _get_email_summary_for_briefing(config):
    """
    Grace-exclusive, best-effort. If the customer has an email-like MCP
    server connected (heuristic name match -- this codebase has no
    fixed, known email MCP contract, since MCP server choice is entirely
    up to the customer), tries to call a plausible "list/search
    messages" tool on it and fold a short raw excerpt into the daily
    briefing's context.

    Deliberately conservative: this is a heuristic on top of an
    unknown, customer-chosen MCP server's actual tool names and
    argument shapes, which vary by provider (Gmail vs Outlook vs
    others) and aren't specified anywhere in this codebase. Any
    mismatch, missing argument, or unexpected response shape just
    results in returning None silently -- this must never break or
    even meaningfully delay the daily briefing over a best-effort
    integration with a tool this file doesn't actually control the
    shape of. Real, honest limitation, not hidden: this may simply not
    work for a given customer's specific email MCP, and that's
    considered an acceptable outcome for a best-effort feature.
    """
    try:
        for server in get_mcp_servers(enabled_only=True):
            name_lower = (server.get("name") or "").lower()
            if not any(hint in name_lower for hint in EMAIL_SERVER_NAME_HINTS):
                continue
            tools = _get_server_tools_cached(server)
            candidate = next(
                (t for t in tools if any(hint in t["name"].lower() for hint in EMAIL_TOOL_NAME_HINTS)),
                None,
            )
            if not candidate:
                continue
            result = call_mcp_tool(server, candidate["name"], {})
            if not result or (isinstance(result, str) and result.startswith("[Error")):
                continue
            text = result if isinstance(result, str) else json.dumps(result)
            return text[:2000]
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[daily briefing] email summary lookup failed (non-fatal, best-effort): {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    return None


def daily_briefing(context):
    """
    Executive daily briefing for every customer, on by default -- runs
    twice a day (9am and 5pm, see com.curant.dailybriefing.plist),
    deliberately a different behavior from proactive_check above, not a
    duplicate of it. proactive_check asks the model to DECIDE whether
    anything warrants a heads-up, and is conservative on purpose ("most
    days should conclude nothing is worth sending"). This instead ALWAYS
    produces a real structured digest (today's agenda + open reminders)
    when there's anything to report, on a fixed schedule, without
    needing something to feel "urgent" first.

    Originally built Grace-exclusive; opened up to every customer by
    default per the owner's direction. config["daily_briefing_enabled"]
    (default True) is the one opt-out lever -- a customer who doesn't
    want it can turn it off locally; there's no server-side tier check
    left gating this anymore.

    Same fail-closed contract as proactive_check: any error, missing
    license, or no API key just results in should_send=false, never a
    broken/partial message.
    """
    init_local_db()  # needed for get_pending_email_sends below (pending_email_sends table)
    config = load_config()
    key = config.get("license_key")
    if not key:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False, "error": "not_activated"}))
        return

    config, active = refresh_status_if_stale(config)
    if not active:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False, "error": "inactive_or_invalid_license"}))
        return

    if not config.get("daily_briefing_enabled", True):
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))
        return

    if _is_quiet_hours_now(config):
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False, "reason": "quiet_hours"}))
        return

    provider = get_provider(config)
    api_key = get_api_key(config, provider)
    if not api_key:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False, "error": "no_api_key_set"}))
        return

    pending_sends = get_pending_email_sends(status="pending")
    # Real fix 2026-08-13: this used to bail out entirely on empty
    # calendar/reminders context, which meant a customer with pending
    # emails awaiting approval (see run_iris_inbox_check /
    # pending_email_sends) but nothing on their calendar today would
    # NEVER hear about the pending queue through the briefing -- it'd
    # just sit there silently until they happened to ask. Pending sends
    # alone are now also a valid reason to still produce a briefing.
    if not context and not pending_sends:
        # Nothing to summarize -- an empty briefing isn't useful, don't send one.
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))
        return

    # Grace-exclusive, best-effort: fold in an email summary if a
    # plausible email MCP server is connected -- see
    # _get_email_summary_for_briefing's docstring for why this is
    # heuristic and allowed to simply not work for a given customer.
    if get_customer_august_tier(config) == "grace":
        email_summary = _get_email_summary_for_briefing(config)
        if email_summary:
            context = context + "\n\nRecent email activity (raw, from a connected email tool):\n" + email_summary

    if pending_sends:
        pending_lines = "\n".join(
            f"#{p['id']}: to {p['to_addr']}, subject \"{p['subject']}\" -- {p['reasoning'] or 'no reasoning given'}"
            for p in pending_sends
        )
        context = (context or "") + (
            "\n\nEmails an unattended background check drafted, awaiting your approval before "
            f"anything sends:\n{pending_lines}"
        )

    memories = get_memories()
    people = get_important_people()
    extra = (
        "Write today's executive daily briefing -- a concise, well-organized morning "
        "digest of the day ahead, based on the live calendar/reminders context below. "
        "Unlike a normal proactive check-in, you are NOT deciding whether this is worth "
        "sending -- always produce a real briefing as long as there's something in the "
        "context to summarize. Keep it tight and scannable: today's agenda first, then "
        "anything open/overdue worth flagging. No filler, no 'let me know if you need "
        "anything else' -- just the briefing itself. If there are pending emails awaiting "
        "approval in the context below, mention them clearly with their id numbers and tell "
        "the customer they can reply e.g. \"approve 1\" or \"reject 2\" or \"approve all\" to act "
        "on them.\n\n"
        f"Today's live context:\n{context}\n\n"
        'Respond with ONLY a JSON object: {"message": "..."}.'
    )
    system_prompt = build_system_prompt(config, memories, people, "", extra_instruction=extra)

    try:
        raw = call_llm(
            provider, api_key, system_prompt,
            messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Write this morning's executive briefing."}],
            max_tokens=600, tier="main",
        ).strip()
        if raw.startswith("```"):
            raw = raw.strip("`")
            if raw.startswith("json"):
                raw = raw[4:]
        result = json.loads(raw)
        message = (result.get("message") or "").strip()
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"Daily briefing failed (non-fatal, not sending): {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))
        return

    if not message:
        print(json.dumps({"should_send": False}))
        return

    save_message("assistant", message)
    print(json.dumps({
        "should_send": True,
        "message": message,
        "reply_format": config.get("reply_format", "text"),
        "voice_tier": config.get("voice_tier", "standard"),
    }))


DEFAULT_IRIS_INBOX_SERVER_NAME = "iris-gmail"


def get_iris_inbox_server_name(config):
    return config.get("iris_inbox_mcp_server") or DEFAULT_IRIS_INBOX_SERVER_NAME


def _get_iris_inbox_check_tools(server_name):
    """The tool list for run_iris_inbox_check() -- deliberately much
    narrower than get_all_available_tools(). Real safety property this
    exists to guarantee: an unattended job with no human watching must
    NEVER be able to call anything that actually sends, deletes, or
    modifies something in a real inbox. Gmail's REST-derived MCP tool
    names are consistent about this -- get_*/list_* are always
    read-only, everything else (create_/update_/patch_/delete_/send_/
    modify_/trash_/untrash_/verify_/insert_/set_default_/add_/remove_/
    batch_/watch_/stop_) has a real side effect -- so filtering on that
    prefix is a genuine safety boundary, not just a naming convention,
    for this specific well-known server. The only way this job can
    propose a reply is queue_email_for_approval, which writes to
    curant-cli's OWN local queue table, never to the real account --
    see process_email_approvals_tool for the only path that can turn a
    queued item into a real sent email, and it requires a live customer
    to trigger it."""
    server = next((s for s in get_mcp_servers(enabled_only=True) if s["name"] == server_name), None)
    if not server:
        return None, []
    all_tools = _get_server_tools_cached(server)
    read_only = [t for t in all_tools if t["name"].startswith(("get_", "list_"))]
    tools = [{
        "qualified_name": f"{server_name}__{t['name']}",
        "description": f"[{server_name}] {t['description']}",
        "input_schema": t["input_schema"],
        "server_command": server,
        "raw_name": t["name"],
        "builtin": False,
    } for t in read_only]
    tools.append({
        "qualified_name": "queue_email_for_approval",
        "description": (
            "Propose a reply or new email based on something you found in the inbox you're checking. "
            "This does NOT send anything -- it queues the draft for the customer to review and approve "
            "later (they'll see it in their next briefing). This is the only way you're able to "
            "propose an outgoing email during this unattended check; there is no send tool available "
            "to you right now, by design."
        ),
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "to": {"type": "string", "description": "recipient email address"},
                "subject": {"type": "string"},
                "body": {"type": "string"},
                "reasoning": {"type": "string", "description": "short explanation of why this reply is worth sending, for the customer's review"},
                "source_context": {"type": "string", "description": "brief excerpt/reference to the email this is responding to"},
            },
            "required": ["to", "subject", "body", "reasoning"],
        },
        "server_command": None,
        "raw_name": "queue_email_for_approval",
        "builtin": True,
        "_iris_server_name": server_name,
    })
    return server, tools


def set_iris_inbox_cmd(name):
